  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nWest of the mountains, temperatures at noon were running 3 to 8 \ndegrees warmer than this time yesterday, likely due to the \ndecreased high cloud coverage today. Low temperatures this morning\nstarted off in the low to mid 70s for the coast and western \nvalleys, further inland lows were in the upper 70s to low 80s. The\nwarmest conditions overnight were in the low deserts where \ntemperatures overnight only fell to the upper 80s or low 90s. \n\nLingering mid-level moisture will bring a slight chance of \nshowers and thunderstorms over the mountains this afternoon. \nSouthwesterly flow aloft may push a few of these storms into the \nlow desert. Latest high resolution guidance shows a decrease in \ncoverage compared to earlier runs, with peak rainfall rates \nexpected to be 0.50-0.70 inches per hour where thunderstorms \ndevelop. \n\nThe upper level ridge will start to get pushed east Saturday as a \ntrough of low pressure moves into the Pacific Northwest. This will \nallow for a few degrees of cooling for afternoon highs, but \novernight lows will remain elevated continuing to limit overnight \nrelief from the heat. Widespread moderate to locally major \nHeatRisk will prevail for the coast, valleys, and San Diego \nmountains. Major HeatRisk will be maintained into Saturday for \nthe low deserts. Given the slower cooling, the heat products for \nthe areas mentioned above have been extended into Saturday. The \ntrough will be the main influence over our weather by Sunday, \nbringing cooler conditions with increased marine layer cloud \ncoverage and an uptick in westerly winds. Both high and low \ntemperatures will finally fall below average. \n\nFor next week, troughing remains the prevailing pattern over the \nwest coast. For the middle of the week, there is a potential for \nincreased clouds and light precipitation due to moisture from \nTropical Storm Karina. The current NHC track keeps the system far \nenough south where this will be the main impacts from the storm \nin addition to an increase in surf. Minor warming is possible late\nin the week as high pressure tries to expand westward again, \nthough the warmest ensemble solutions only account for about 40% \nof the ensemble space at this point, with the remaining 60% \nkeeping temperatures closer to midweek values.",
